The crate is imported as `adw` (standard). Now let me write the implementation. Let me plan the exact edits to `lib.rs`.

1. Add `adw` to imports.
2. Add `WidgetKind::Navigation`.
3. `widget_kind`: a stack with ROUTE → Navigation.
4. `build_widget`: Navigation → build AdwNavigationView.
5. Add fields to GtkRenderer:
   - `nav_views: HashMap<u32, adw::NavigationView>` — but actually the slot widget returned by build_widget can be downcast. I need the NavigationView handle to push/pop pages. I could store the nav view per slot id.
   - `nav_back_attached: HashSet<u32>` — per-slot guard for connect_popped.
   - `nav_reconciling: HashMap<u32, Rc<Cell<bool>>>` — reconciling flag per slot, shared with the closure.
   - Also maybe `nav_visible_route: HashMap<u32, String>` to detect route changes? The reconcile algorithm via find_page/pop_to_page is stateless enough; but I need to know when the destination changed to decide push vs pop. Let me think again.

Reconcile algorithm for a nav slot with current route R and current child widget W:
```
let Some(nav) = self.nav_views.get(&slot_id).cloned() else { return };
let Some(target_widget) = children.first().and_then(|id| self.widgets.get(id).cloned()) else { return };
let route = node.string_property(ROUTE).unwrap_or_default();
let reconciling = self.nav_reconciling.get(&slot_id).cloned();

// ensure connect_popped attached once
if !self.nav_back_attached.contains(&slot_id) {
    attach...
    self.nav_back_attached.insert(slot_id);
}

if let Some(page) = nav.find_page(&route) {
    // update content (the destination widget may be freshly rebuilt)
    page.set_child(Some(&target_widget));
    if nav.visible_page().as_ref() != Some(page) {
        reconciling.set(true);
        nav.pop_to_page(&page);
        reconciling.set(false);
    }
} else {
    let page = nav_page(&route, &route, &target_widget);
    reconciling.set(true);   // push doesn't emit popped, but harmless
    nav.push(&page);
    reconciling.set(false);
}
```

Wait — there's an important subtlety with `find_page(&route)`: `find_page` searches by tag. Let me confirm find_page signature: `fn find_page(&self, tag: &str) -> Option<NavigationPage>`. Yes.

But `pop_to_page(&page)` when the page IS visible returns false and does nothing (no transition). Good — but we already set_child, so content updates in place. 

Edge: what about when the route is empty (ROUTE not emitted)? A NavigationContainer always emits ROUTE per spec. But a `Conditional.when` slot also might carry ROUTE? No — only NavigationContainer sets ROUTE. So promotion is keyed on ROUTE presence, and the slot should always have ROUTE. But defensively: if route is empty, use the child id as tag? Let me fall back to a tag derived from child id so pages still work. Actually if ROUTE is empty, fall back to treating it like an in-place swap? Simpler: if route empty, tag = format!("{}", child_id). That keeps it working.

Hmm, but a cleaner approach: don't require ROUTE presence for the *reconcile* to work; require it only for *promotion* (widget_kind). Since promotion requires ROUTE, the route is present. I'll still guard.

Now `nav_page` builder:
```
fn nav_page(title: &str, tag: &str, child: &gtk::Widget) -> adw::NavigationPage {
    let toolbar = adw::ToolbarView::new();
    let header = adw::HeaderBar::new();
    header.set_show_back_button(true);
    header.set_show_start_title_buttons(false);
    header.set_show_end_title_buttons(false);
    toolbar.add_top_bar(&header);
    toolbar.set_content(Some(child));
    adw::NavigationPage::builder()
        .title(title)
        .tag(tag)
        .child(&toolbar)
        .build()
}
```

The `connect_popped` attachment:
```
fn attach_nav_back(&mut self, slot_id: u32, nav: &adw::NavigationView) {
    if self.nav_back_attached.contains(&slot_id) { return; }
    let reconciling = Rc::new(Cell::new(false));
    self.nav_reconciling.insert(slot_id, reconciling.clone());
    let Some(sink) = self.event_sink.clone() else { return; };
    nav.connect_popped(move |_nav, _page| {
        if !reconciling.get() {
            sink.borrow_mut()(Event::Navigate { url: None });
        }
    });
    self.nav_back_attached.insert(slot_id);
}
```

Wait — `connect_popped` fires when a page is popped. When the user clicks the header-bar back button, the nav view pops the page → `popped` fires → emit NAVIGATE (no URL) → app pops. Good. When we programmatically pop (pop_to_page) during reconcile, `popped` fires but reconciling guard suppresses. 

But `pop_to_page` pops MULTIPLE pages, firing `popped` for each, all suppressed. Good.

Edge: When app-initiated pop happens (Escape → NAVIGATE → app pops → re-emits A), the app re-emits A's subtree + ROUTE="/a". Reconcile: find_page("/a") exists (it's below) → set_child(new A widget) → pop_to_page(page A) → pops B (suppressed) → visible A. 

But WAIT: the app re-emits A only AFTER receiving NAVIGATE from our Escape handler — the Escape handler is at window level and ALWAYS emits NAVIGATE regardless of nav state. But there's a double-pop concern: Escape → NAVIGATE → app pops its back-stack → re-emits A. Meanwhile, was there a header-bar pop also? No, user pressed Escape not the button. Fine.

Another subtlety: When the app *pushes* B, the reconcile pops nothing, pushes a page. Good. When the app does `replace()` (route R1 → R2, same depth), find_page(R2) likely doesn't exist → push. Depth grows by 1 (extra page). Documented limitation. Alternatively, I could detect "the current visible page's route != new route AND there are pages above the previous visible" ... too fragile. Accept push behavior. Actually, hmm, I could improve: track last visible route per slot. On route change:
- if last_visible_route == route → no-op
- else if find_page(route) exists → pop_to_page
- else if we can determine replace? No signal. 

I'll keep it simple. But one concern: on the very first mount, the slot may emit multiple frames (initial ROUTE + destination swap could be separate frames? In Java, ROUTE coalesced into the same frame as the destination swap). The reconcile handles any order: if the slot is first seen with route "/" + child → push page "/". If a later frame changes route → pop/push accordingly.

