Now I'll refactor `Router` to support binding to an external `WritableSignal<String>`. Design:

- Keep the internal `current` `WritableSignal<Route>` for the unbound case.
- Add a bound variant: `Router(RouteTable table, WritableSignal<String> activePath)`.
  - `current` becomes a derived signal: a `WritableSignal<Route>` that reads/writes the string signal? Or keep `current` and mirror.

Let me think about the cleanest implementation that keeps `current` (WritableSignal<Route>) as the read/write source but syncs with the external string signal.

Approach: keep `current = Signals.signal(Route.of(activePath.get() != null ? activePath.get() : "/"))` initialized from the external signal. Add:
- A subscriber effect: `Signals.effect(() -> { String p = activePath.get(); if (!selfWriting && p != null) go(Route.of(p)); })` — external writes → guard-aware.
- `go(Route)`: write `current` AND mirror to `activePath` (selfWriting-guarded): `current.set(route); if (bound) { selfWriting = true; activePath.set(route.pathOnly()); selfWriting = false; }`.

Wait — mirroring: when the app navigates (`go` → `current.set` + `activePath.set`), the subscriber effect fires (activePath changed) but selfWriting is true → skips. Good.

But there's a subtlety: `current` is the internal signal the container watches (`router::destination` → `path()` → `current.get().pathOnly()`). If I mirror to activePath on every `go`, and the subscriber re-routes external activePath changes through `go` → `current.set` — then for an EXTERNAL write (host sets activePath): subscriber fires → `go(path)` → guard match → `current.set(...)` + mirror `activePath.set(...)` (selfWriting true during mirror → subscriber skips). So the host's `activePath.set(url)` → subscriber → go → current.set + activePath.set(redirect-or-path). Both current and activePath now reflect the final path. 

But the host set activePath to `/x`; if no guard redirect, `go(/x)` sets current to /x and mirrors activePath to /x (same value → suppressed). Fine. If guard redirects to /y: current=/y, activePath=/y. Good.

Self-writes: `router.navigate("/a")` → `go(/a)` → current=/a + activePath=/a (selfWriting true during set → subscriber skips). Good, no loop.

Equality suppression: `go` on same path → `current.set` suppressed; mirror `activePath.set` suppressed. Subscriber not re-fired. Good.

Initial: constructor with bound signal → `current = Signals.signal(Route.of(activePath.get()))` — wait, but the initial guard pass must run. Let me do: after setting up current + subscriber, call `go(Route.of(activePath.get()))` to apply guards on the initial path. But `go` writes current (maybe same) + mirrors. The subscriber effect — if registered BEFORE the initial go, the initial go's activePath.set (if redirect) fires subscriber with selfWriting=true → skip. Good. If no redirect, activePath.set same → suppressed. Good.

Hmm, but the subscriber effect's FIRST run (on registration) reads `activePath.get()` immediately. If I register it before the initial go, the first run fires with the initial path, selfWriting=false → calls go(initial) → guard pass. That IS the initial guard pass (via the effect). Then I shouldn't ALSO call go in the constructor. So: register the subscriber effect, and its initial run does the guard pass. But the effect's initial run calling `go` which sets activePath (selfWriting) — re-entrant during effect? Let me be careful.

Actually, cleaner: initialize `current` from the signal, register the subscriber effect (which does the guard pass on its initial run and on external changes), and DON'T call go separately. But the effect's initial run → go(initial) → guard match → current.set(redirect) + mirror activePath.set(redirect) (selfWriting). During `go`, `current.set` triggers the container's structural effect? The container isn't mounted yet (constructor runs in body() during mount render). The structural effect registers later. So current.set at construction just sets the value; no listeners yet. Fine.

But wait — the subscriber effect and the guard: on the initial run, if there's no redirect, go(initial) sets current to initial (already the value) → suppressed. No-op. If redirect, current + activePath get the redirect. Good — initial guard pass works.

Re-entrancy concern: the effect's initial run calling `go` which does `activePath.set` → the effect re-fires (synchronously?) because activePath changed → selfWriting=true during the set → skips. After go returns, selfWriting=false. The effect's initial run completes. Good.

But does `activePath.set` inside the effect (during the effect's own execution) cause the effect to re-run synchronously (nested)? The signal library flushes synchronously at the end of a write. If the write happens during an effect flush... nested flush. The existing code does `current.set` in go which triggers the structural effect synchronously. So nested writes are supported. Let me trust the signal library handles it (it flushes synchronously; a write during a flush might schedule/defer). Actually — `Signals.effect` with default options: does a write during the effect's own run re-run it? The selfWriting guard prevents the re-route logic. The re-fire would call go again with selfWriting=true → skip (returns immediately). So even if re-fired, it's a no-op. Safe.

Hmm, but there's a subtle issue: the subscriber effect reads `activePath.get()` and calls `go`. During `go`, it writes `activePath` (mirror) with selfWriting=true. If the effect re-fires synchronously from that write, it reads activePath.get() (the new value), selfWriting=true → skip. Good.

Now — one more: `current` is `WritableSignal<Route>`. The bound constructor initializes it from activePath.get(). But what if activePath is null (host didn't provide)? Then default "/". The bound router is only used when the signal is provided. I'll require non-null signal (Objects.requireNonNull) and read `.get()`; if the signal's value is null → default "/".

Let me also handle: `pathOf` for external URLs — the subscriber gets `activePath.get()` which the host sets to a URL (e.g., "https://...") from NAVIGATE events. `go(Route.of(p))` → `table.match(Route.of(p).pathOnly())` — `Route.of(p)` requires absolute path starting with "/"! A full URL "https://host/u/7" would throw (Route constructor checks startsWith "/"). So the subscriber must strip to pathOnly first: `go(Route.of(Route.of(p).pathOnly()))`? `Route.of(p)` throws for non-absolute. Use `handlePlatformNavigation(p)` instead which does `pathOf(url)` → pathOnly → go. So the subscriber should call `handlePlatformNavigation(p)` (guard-aware + URL-normalizing), not `go(Route.of(p))`.

Actually — the host sets activePath from `env.route()` (absolute path) and from NAVIGATE urls (could be full URLs). `handlePlatformNavigation` handles both (pathOf strips). So subscriber → `handlePlatformNavigation(p)`. But `handlePlatformNavigation` calls `go(Route.of(pathOf(url)))`. Good.

Wait — but `handlePlatformNavigation` doesn't touch the back-stack (correct for platform nav). And `go` is also used by navigate/push. The subscriber uses handlePlatformNavigation for external changes. Good.

Let me also reconsider: should external writes to activePath be treated as "platform navigation" (no back-stack change) or "navigate"? The host sets activePath from env.route() (deep link / popstate) — platform navigation semantics (no stack change). And NAVIGATE URLs — also platform. So `handlePlatformNavigation` is correct (no back-stack change, guard-aware).

But there's the initial seed: the host creates `activePath = Signals.signal(env.route())` before mount. The router subscribes → initial run → handlePlatformNavigation(initialPath) → guard-aware. Matches current `navigate(initialPath)` behavior (guards run, no stack change — navigate doesn't touch stack either). Good.

Now the mirroring in `go`: when the APP navigates (push/navigate/replace/pop), we write current AND mirror to activePath (so the app's navigation is reflected in the activePath signal that the host/app observe). That's important — the activePath is the single source of truth for "current path". So `go` mirrors.

But `pop`: `go(stack.pop())` → mirror → activePath = popped path. Good — the activePath tracks the router.

Let me now write the Router changes:

```java
public final class Router {
    private final RouteTable table;
    private final Deque<Route> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final WritableSignal<Route> current;
    private final WritableSignal<String> boundPath;   // null when unbound
    private boolean selfWriting;

    public Router(RouteTable table) {
        this.table = Objects.requireNonNull(table, "table");
        this.current = Signals.signal(Route.of("/"));
        this.boundPath = null;
    }

    /** A router whose current route is bound to an external active-path signal
     *  (Platform.ACTIVE_PATH): external writes are routed through guards, and the
     *  router's own navigation is mirrored back into the signal. */
    public Router(RouteTable table, WritableSignal<String> activePath) {
        this.table = Objects.requireNonNull(table, "table");
        Objects.requireNonNull(activePath, "activePath");
        String initial = activePath.get();
        this.current = Signals.signal(Route.of(initial != null ? Route.of(initial).pathOnly() : "/"));
        this.boundPath = activePath;
        // External writes to the signal are platform navigation: re-route guard-aware.
        Signals.effect(() -> {
            String path = activePath.get();
            if (!selfWriting && path != null) {
                handlePlatformNavigation(path);
            }
        });
    }
    ...
    private void go(Route route) {
        RouteTable.RouteMatch match = table.match(route.path()).orElse(null);
        if (match != null && match.isRedirect()) {
            setCurrent(Route.of(match.redirect()));
            return;
        }
        setCurrent(route);
    }

    private void setCurrent(Route route) {
        current.set(route);
        if (boundPath != null) {
            selfWriting = true;
            try { boundPath.set(route.pathOnly()); } finally { selfWriting = false; }
        }
    }
```

Wait — `Route.of(initial).pathOnly()` for the initial — if initial is a full URL, Route.of throws. The host seeds env.route() which is a path (absolute). But to be safe, use a normalizer. Let me add a static helper: `normalizePath(String)` that handles both absolute paths and URLs → pathOnly. Actually `pathOf` handles URLs. Let me use `pathOf(initial)` for the initial too (it handles absolute paths and URLs). But `pathOf` is `static` — accessible. Let me use it: `Route.of(pathOf(initial))`.

Hmm wait, `pathOf` returns pathOnly (strips query). For "/users/42?q=1" → "/users/42". Good.

Let me also double-check the subscriber effect's re-entrancy with `handlePlatformNavigation` → `go` → `setCurrent` → `boundPath.set` (selfWriting=true) → the subscriber effect re-fires? If the signal library re-fires synchronously during set, selfWriting=true → skip. If it defers to end-of-write, selfWriting is false by then → the effect re-runs → reads activePath.get() (the new value) → calls handlePlatformNavigation again → go → current.set(same) suppressed + boundPath.set(same) suppressed → no-op. So either way safe. Good.

Actually — a concern: after an EXTERNAL write (host sets activePath="/x"), the subscriber runs handlePlatformNavigation("/x") → go → if no guard, setCurrent(Route.of("/x")) → current.set(/x) + boundPath.set("/x") (selfWriting true). But activePath is ALREADY "/x" (the host set it) → boundPath.set("/x") suppressed. Good — no mirror write needed. If guard redirects to /y: current=/y, boundPath=/y (selfWriting). activePath now /y. The host's original "/x" is overwritten by /y (the redirect target). The app observes /y. Correct (matches current behavior where a guarded deep link redirects).

Now — `navigate`, `push`, `replace`, `pop` all call `go` → `setCurrent` → mirror. Good.

`routeSignal()` returns `current.asReadonly()`. `current()` returns `current.get()`. `path()` returns `current().pathOnly()`. All unchanged.

`handleEvent` unchanged (back + URL). For a bound router, the host no longer forwards URL-NAVIGATE to handleEvent (it sets the signal instead), but handleEvent's URL branch remains for compat.

Let me write the Router edits.

Also `Navigation.navigator().build(WritableSignal<String>)`:
```java
public Router build(WritableSignal<String> activePath) {
    return new Router(table.build(), activePath);
}
public Router build() {
    return new Router(table.build());
}
```
But the current `Builder.build()` returns `new Router(table.build())` and `navigator(initialPath)` seeds via `router.navigate(initialPath)`. Let me check the current Builder. `navigator()` → Builder("/"), `navigator(initialPath)` → Builder(initialPath), `build()` → new Router(table.build()) + router.navigate(initialPath). 

For the bound case: `build(WritableSignal<String>)` → new Router(table.build(), signal) (no navigate seeding — the router's subscriber does the guard pass on the signal's current value). So the bound build ignores the initialPath? The signal already carries the initial path (host seeded it). So `navigator()` (default "/") + `.build(activePath)` — the signal's value is the real initial. The Builder's `initialPath` for the bound build should be ignored (or the Builder default). Let me have `build(activePath)` ignore initialPath and rely on the signal.

Hmm, but `navigator("/kitchen")` in the bound case would seed "/kitchen" then bound to the signal... conflicting. The bound build should use the signal's value. So `build(activePath)` ignores the Builder's initialPath. Document that.

Let me write Navigation's Builder changes.

Now — the bound router in SplitNavDemo.body():
```java
Router router = Navigation.navigator()
    .route("/", new HomeView()) ...
    .fallback(Text.of("Not Found"))
    .build(activePath);
```
The host provides activePath seeded with env.route(). Good.

Now the host SessionApps:
```java
WritableSignal<String> activePath = Signals.signal(env.route());
RenderResult result = emitter.mount(
        new SplitNavDemo().environment(Platform.ACTIVE_PATH, activePath),
        new Environment(state));
```
Wait — the host mounts the demo root. But which root? Currently `SplitNavDemo.app(env.route()).root()`. Now: `new SplitNavDemo().environment(Platform.ACTIVE_PATH, activePath)`. But the SplitNavDemo is in the demo-views module; the host imports SplitNavDemo. Fine.

`applyEnvironment`:
```java
void applyEnvironment(EnvironmentData env) {
    activePath.set(env.route());
    // viewport
}
```
Wait — should `applyEnvironment`'s route re-route still happen? Yes — the env carries the route; setting the signal → bound router's subscriber → guard-aware. But careful: on SSR, the host constructs activePath with env.route() (the initial), so applyEnvironment's `activePath.set(env.route())` at the end of construction is a no-op (same value). Later env messages (viewport resize) re-emit route (same pathname) → no-op. Deep-link env messages → updates. Good.

`dispatch` — NAVIGATE handling:
```java
} else if (event.isNavigate()) {
    if (event.url() != null) {
        activePath.set(event.url());   // platform path change → signal → bound router guards
    } else {
        Consumer<Event> sink = navigateHandler;   // back
        if (sink != null) sink.accept(event);
    }
}
```

Wait — but the host needs `activePath` accessible in dispatch. Add a field. And `navigateHandler` stays.

Let me now write all the edits. Let me start with Router.

Actually — let me reconsider the `selfWriting` shared between go's mirror and the subscriber. The subscriber checks `!selfWriting`. The mirror sets selfWriting around boundPath.set. But what if a NAVIGATE-back (pop) is in flight and selfWriting... no, selfWriting is only true during mirror set. Fine.

One more: `push` does `stack.push(current()); go(Route.of(path))`. `current()` reads `current.get()`. Fine.

Let me also double check: does anything external read `Router.current` or rely on `routeSignal()`? The `NavigationContainer` uses `router::destination` and `router::path`. `Navigation.isActive` uses `router.path()`. `Navigation.ROUTER` env scoping uses the router instance. So the internal `current` change to be bound is transparent. Good.

Let me write Router edits now.

