Found it. The DOM client's `click` handler (which sends POINTER_UP, the event that fires the tap action in `InputDispatcher`):

```js
document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    const target = event.target as Element | null;
    if (!target) return;
    const button = nodeOf(target);
    if (button && button.matches("button") && transport.open && listens(button, LISTEN_POINTER_UP)) {
        // ... send POINTER_UP
        return;
    }
    // ... stepper handling ...
```

So the POINTER_UP event is only sent when the closest `[data-pathland-id]` element **`matches("button")`**. The new menu row is a `VStack` → rendered as a `<div>`, not a `<button>`. So `button.matches("button")` is false → no POINTER_UP sent → the tap action never fires.

Also, `pointerdown` IS sent for any node with the POINTER_DOWN listener bit (the VStack has it) — so POINTER_DOWN works, but POINTER_UP (click) is gated to `<button>` elements only.

So the root cause: the DOM client's click handler only emits POINTER_UP for `<button>` elements. A tap gesture on a non-button element (VStack/div, Text/span) never produces POINTER_UP → the server's `InputDispatcher` (which fires tapActions on pointer-up) never fires.

Let me verify the rest of the click handler to be sure (maybe there's a generic fallback). Let me read lines 193-240.

Also, let me double-check: the `InputDispatcher` fires tapActions on `event.isPointerUp()`. And `TapGestureView` sets POINTER_DOWN|POINTER_UP listeners. So the server expects a POINTER_UP for the node. The client only sends POINTER_UP for buttons. So yes.

Additionally, on the GTK/native side, does the tap work for non-buttons? Let me check the GTK renderer's event handling — but the user is likely testing on the web (SSR demo). Let me focus on the web path but also check GTK.

Let me read the rest of the click handler.

