Both reconciles read the style correctly (BorderedButtonStyle) inside the render block! So the destination IS rendered styled. But the SECOND frame doesn't contain the props!

So the props ARE applied to the fresh button node, but `reconcileChildren` doesn't emit them. Why? Because `reconcileChildren(slot, oldChildren, newChildren, ops)` compares the old button node (id X) and new button node (id Y?) and the reconciliation logic decides the new node EQUALS the old node structurally? No...

Wait — the fresh button node: does it have props? Let me check: Button.render applies `node.component = Components.BUTTON` after `body.render(env)` where body = BorderedButtonStyle.makeBody(...) = label with modifiers (ForegroundStyle, Padding, Background, Border) + TapGesture. So `body.render(env)` produces a node with properties (COLOR, PADDING, BACKGROUND_COLOR, BORDER_WIDTH, BORDER_COLOR, BORDER_RADIUS) and listeners. Then component overwritten to BUTTON. So the fresh node HAS the props.

Then reconcileChildren diffs old (button "One" node, id e.g. 5) vs new (button "Two" node, id 6). The reconciliation: since it's a fresh node (different identity), it should CREATE the new node + emit its props, and REMOVE the old. But the frame shows only SET_TEXT + ROUTE — no CREATE.

Hmm. So maybe reconcileChildren has special logic: if the old and new children are "compatible" (same component type?), it reuses the old node's id and only emits property diffs. Let me look at reconcileChildren.

Actually, the second frame shows SET_TEXT (b=00000000 — wait, `b=00000000` with SET_TEXT command cmd=03). SET_TEXT opcode: b holds... the text string offset? `b=00000000` → string offset 0? Hmm the SET_TEXT for the button label. And there's no SET_PROPERTY for the props. 

Actually wait — if reconcileChildren reuses the old node id for the new button (same slot, compatible types), it would diff the old node's props vs new node's props. Old button node id 5 has props {COLOR, PADDING, BACKGROUND_COLOR, BORDER_*, EVENT_LISTENERS}. New button node id 6 also has the SAME props (same BorderedButtonStyle). So the diff would emit NOTHING for props (they're equal!), only SET_TEXT for the label change ("One" → "Two"). 

That's exactly what we see! So the destination IS styled — the props are just NOT re-emitted because they're unchanged from the previous destination (both buttons use BorderedButtonStyle with identical values).

So my TEST assertion is wrong: `hasBackgroundColor(second)` fails not because the style is lost, but because the props are diffed-away (already present on the reused node). The behavior is CORRECT — the second destination IS styled, it just doesn't re-emit identical props.

So my regression test needs a better assertion. The real bug (pre-fix) would manifest if the new destination rendered PLAIN — then the props would NOT be equal to old (plain has no props), and the diff would... hmm, actually if the old node had props and new is plain, the diff would emit REMOVE/clearing? How does the emitter clear a property? Probably via a value reset. Anyway.

The correct regression test: verify that the new destination's button node in the retained tree has the style props, OR verify the rendered style is what's read. Since `sink` only shows deltas, I need a different check.

Better approach for the regression test: make the two destinations use DIFFERENT styles so a lost style would show as a delta. E.g.:
- route "/" → Button styled with BorderedButtonStyle.INSTANCE (via app-level `.buttonStyle` scope).
- route "/two" → Button with the SAME scoped style.

Hmm, both use the same scoped style so identical.

Alternative: assert on the RETAINED tree instead of the frame. But the emitter's tree is private. 

Alternative: check that after navigate, the button's props equal the previous button's props (i.e., style retained) — can't easily without tree access.

Better alternative: make the check meaningful via the frame: use a destination whose Button is at a DIFFERENT position or a NEW structure so reconcile creates fresh nodes and emits props. Actually simplest: make the second destination a VStack containing a Button (different structure → new node → CREATE emitted → props emitted). But then props are emitted because it's a brand-new node.

Actually, the cleanest regression test: verify the destination still reads the style by making the route destinations record what style `env.buttonStyle()` returns at render. Like the DebugStyle debug — capture the style read during the second destination's render. Let me write the test with a probe destination:

```java
@Test
void buttonStyleScopedAboveAStructuralSlotSurvivesReRender() {
    java.util.List<ButtonStyle> seen = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
    Router router = Navigation.navigator()
            .route("/", p -> probeButton(seen, "One"))
            .route("/two", p -> probeButton(seen, "Two"))
            .build();
    FrameOpcodeSink sink = sink();
    new Emitter(sink).mount(
            NavigationContainer.of(router).modifier(ButtonStyleMod.of(BorderedButtonStyle.INSTANCE)),
            Environment.DEFAULT);
    router.navigate("/two");
    assertEquals(2, seen.size());
    assertEquals(BorderedButtonStyle.INSTANCE, seen.get(0), "initial destination sees the scoped style");
    assertEquals(BorderedButtonStyle.INSTANCE, seen.get(1), "a destination re-rendered via the slot keeps the scoped style");
}
```
where probeButton is:
```java
private static View probeButton(java.util.List<ButtonStyle> seen, String label) {
    return new View() {
        @Override
        public com.pathland.view.emit.PathlandNode render(Environment env) {
            seen.add(env.buttonStyle());
            return Button.of(label, () -> { }).render(env);
        }
    };
}
```

This directly asserts the style survives the slot re-render — exactly the regression. And it works because it reads at render time (like a destination author would).

Wait — but the destinations are rendered at mount AND at reconcile. Mount renders "/" → seen[0]. Navigate → reconcile renders "/two" → seen[1]. Good.

But careful: the immediate first effect run at registration ALSO renders "/" again → seen would get a third entry at mount time! Because the structural effect runs reconcileSlot immediately at registration, which renders the destination again. So seen would be [style, style, style] (mount destination render at NavigationContainer.render, then effect's immediate reconcile, then navigate's reconcile). Actually:

1. Mount: `root.render(env)` → NavigationContainer.render → `router.destination()` reads "/" → renders probeButton("One") → seen[0].
2. registerBindings → effect immediate run → reconcileSlot → renders "/" again → seen[1].
3. navigate("/two") → effect re-run → reconcileSlot → renders "/two" → seen[2].

So seen.size() would be 3. The last is the "/two" destination. I could assert `seen.get(seen.size()-1) == BorderedButtonStyle.INSTANCE` and size >= 3. Or clear seen after mount and check only the navigate render. Better:

```java
new Emitter(sink).mount(...);  // mount may render destination 1-2 times
seen.clear();
router.navigate("/two");
assertEquals(1, seen.size());
assertEquals(BorderedButtonStyle.INSTANCE, seen.get(0));
```
That's clean and robust.

Actually wait — could the immediate effect run + mount render produce more than one destination render? Mount NavigationContainer.render renders "/" (1), then effect immediate run renders "/" (2). So after mount, seen has 2 entries. Clear. Then navigate → 1 entry. 

Let me simplify: I'll keep the debug out of the main source (remove the debug prints from Emitter.java), and write the test properly.

Let me also reconsider: is the frame-based approach acceptable instead? The delta-based test with identical props can't distinguish. The probe approach directly tests the regression (style visible at render inside the slot). I'll go with the probe.

Let me remove the debug prints from Emitter.java first.

