The KitchenSinkViewTest asserts:
- text inputs >= 2
- value inputs >= 8
- specific components present
- design tokens

Adding a `LabelSection` doesn't break any of these (they're `>=` checks and component-presence checks). It adds IMAGE components (already there via Image elsewhere? The component list doesn't include IMAGE, but that's fine). So a LabelSection with static + reactive labels would:
- Add SET_TEXT entries (fine)
- Add IMAGE nodes (fine)
- A reactive label bound to a signal would register... a signal — if we bind via `Signals.signal("...")` in the section, it doesn't add text/value inputs. Fine.
- No test breakage expected. Optionally add an assertion for the Label text. I'll add a small assertion to KitchenSinkViewTest (e.g., anySetText for the label title, and IMAGE present).

Now let me finalize the plan. I'll present the updated, complete plan and then... The user is in plan mode; I present the final plan for approval. I shouldn't ask more questions unless needed. I have all decisions:
1. Reactive overload: yes
2. Natural size: yes
3. Demo: yes

Let me write the final comprehensive plan.

One more thing to nail down: the reactive overload design details:

`Label` internal:
```java
public final class Label implements View {
    private final String title; private final Signal<String> titleSignal;
    private final String icon; private final Signal<String> iconSignal;

    private Label(String title, Signal<String> titleSignal, String icon, Signal<String> iconSignal) {...}

    public static Label of(String title) { return new Label(title, null, null, null); }
    public static Label of(String title, String icon) { return new Label(title, null, icon, null); }
    public static Label of(Signal<String> title) { return new Label(null, title, null, null); }
    public static Label of(Signal<String> title, Signal<String> icon) { return new Label(null, title, null, icon); }

    @Override
    public View body() {
        LabelStyle style = Environment.value(Environment.LABEL_STYLE).get();  // null → TitleAndIcon
        String title = resolve(title, titleSignal);
        String icon = resolve(icon, iconSignal);
        boolean showIcon = nonBlank(icon) && style.showsIcon();
        boolean showTitle = nonBlank(title) && style.showsTitle();
        List<View> children = new ArrayList<>();
        if (showIcon) children.add(iconSignal != null ? Image.of(iconSignal) : Image.of(icon));
        if (showTitle) children.add(titleSignal != null ? Text.of(titleSignal).modifier(LineLimit.of(1)) : Text.of(title).modifier(LineLimit.of(1)));
        View stack = HStack.of(Alignment.CENTER, 2f, children);
        if (nonBlank(title)) stack = stack.modifier(titleSignal != null ? AccessibilityLabel.of(titleSignal) : AccessibilityLabel.of(title));
        return stack;
    }
}
```

Hmm — `body()` reading `Environment.value(...)` and signal `.get()` — during mount render, not in an effect; but there's a subtlety: the default `View.render` calls `state.connect(this)` then `body()`. Fine.

Wait, an important subtlety with `body()` + `Text.of(signal)`: the emitter's `registerBindings` will register a text binding for the Text node → fine-grained SET_TEXT. And the AccessibilityLabel signal → property binding on the HStack node → SET_PROPERTY(LABEL). And Image signal → SET_PROPERTY(IMAGE_SOURCE). All good.

Edge: `LabelStyle` default when unset: `Environment.value(LABEL_STYLE).get()` returns null → fallback to TitleAndIcon. Should I add `Environment.labelStyle()` accessor (mirroring buttonStyle()) returning the default? Yes, cleaner: `env.labelStyle()`. But body() reads via `Environment.value` (static) not via env object. Actually buttonStyle is read via `env.buttonStyle()` (instance method) in Button. For consistency, Label could read via the static `Environment.value(Environment.LABEL_STYLE)` since body() doesn't receive env. Hmm — body() can't access the env object param. The RouterTest body test uses `Environment.value(...)` static. So use the static path with default fallback. I'll still add `Environment.labelStyle()` instance accessor for API symmetry, but Label uses the static value read in body(). Actually simpler: just the static key + fallback in Label. Do I need the accessor? For API completeness/symmetry with buttonStyle(), adding `labelStyle()` is nice but Label itself will read `Environment.value(LABEL_STYLE).get()` and fall back. I'll include the accessor for parity but note Label uses the static read (body() has no env). Hmm, actually — do we even need the accessor? Not strictly. But it keeps `Environment` symmetric and future composites/controls can use it. I'll include it (small).

Alternatively — override `render(Environment)` instead of `body()`, so we get the env object and could call `env.labelStyle()`. But render-based composition means manually building the HStack node. Body() is cleaner. Keep body() + static read. I'll add the accessor anyway for API completeness? Let me not over-decide; plan will say: `Environment.LABEL_STYLE` key + `labelStyle()` accessor with default (for parity with `buttonStyle()`); `Label.body()` reads it via `Environment.value(...)`.

`LabelStyle` enum:
```java
public enum LabelStyle {
    TITLE_AND_ICON, TITLE_ONLY, ICON_ONLY;
    boolean showsIcon() {...} boolean showsTitle() {...}
}
```
Naming: Java enum constants conventionally UPPER_SNAKE: `TITLE_AND_ICON`, `TITLE_ONLY`, `ICON_ONLY`. Angular uses 'titleAndIcon' etc. DSL.md table can show canonical `TitleAndIconLabelStyle` etc. Java DSL uses `LabelStyle.TITLE_AND_ICON`.

`LabelStyleMod`:
```java
public final class LabelStyleMod implements ViewModifier {
    private final LabelStyle style;
    public static LabelStyleMod of(LabelStyle style) {...}
    @Override public View body(View content) { return content.environment(Environment.LABEL_STYLE, style); }
}
```

`Image.of(Signal<String>)`: add signal field; render puts IMAGE_SOURCE property + propertyBinding when reactive.

`AccessibilityLabel.of(Signal<String>)`: add signal overload → Modified.prop(Properties.LABEL, signal).

Tests (LabelTest in pathland-view):
1. `defaultLabelRendersTitleAndIcon` — Label.of("Save","save.svg") → frame has HSTACK node with SPACING=2, ALIGNMENT=CENTER, LABEL="Save"; children IMAGE (IMAGE_SOURCE "save.svg") + TEXT ("Save") with LINE_LIMIT=1.
2. `titleOnlyStyleDropsTheIcon` — `.modifier(LabelStyleMod.of(TITLE_ONLY))` → only TEXT, no IMAGE; LABEL still "Save".
3. `iconOnlyStyleDropsTheTitleButKeepsTheA11yLabel` — TITLE... ICON_ONLY → only IMAGE; LABEL="Save".
4. `blankTitleAndIconAreSuppressed` — Label.of("", "") → no TEXT/IMAGE children; no LABEL property.
5. `blankTitleSuppressesTextAndLabel` — Label.of("", "save.svg") → IMAGE only, no LABEL.
6. `labelStyleIsAKeyedEnvironmentValue` — read via Environment.value(LABEL_STYLE); inner override wins (nearest-wins); default TitleAndIcon when unset. Mirror the buttonStyle env test.
7. `reactiveTitleReEmitsTextAndLabel` — Label.of(titleSignal) → mount; titleSignal.set("New") → frame2 has SET_TEXT for "New" and SET_PROPERTY(LABEL) "New". (Signal writes are synchronous flush.)
8. `reactiveIconReEmitsImageSource` — Label.of("Save", iconSignal) → iconSignal.set("new.svg") → SET_PROPERTY(IMAGE_SOURCE) "new.svg".
9. `labelStyleSurvivesAStructuralSlotReRender` (optional but good) — mirror the buttonStyle slot test.

